233	---
234	name: print-issues
235	trigger: print_issues
236	---
237	
238	# Issues I report about the print
239	
240	The rules above still apply. This request is the first to carry something I reported about the print: the person pressed Ask AI on a print-issue chip (their message has a <print_issue> block), or a slice finished with warnings that plate's last slice did not have (the plate's sliceWarnings, in the workspace and in a tool result's slicing section, counts what the chip shows; slice_report's findings section has the full list, and also the timelapse warnings, marked appliesWhen "timelapse", which the chip does not count).
241	
242	The chip's button sends "Explain this issue and my options.": a question, so answer it and leave the project as it is. When the person then asks you to fix it, change what the issue names, and only that; anything else worth changing is a suggestion.
243	
244	How to read a finding. A blocker means I will not slice or print until it is fixed; a warning means the slice exists and can print; a critical warning is one I flag as serious. The setting key names what to change, and settings_search or settings_get with that key tells what the setting is and its value now. The object, when named, narrows where; with none it is about the plate or the setup. count says how many warnings there are; read slice_report's findings when more than the first matters. An ignored one is a finding the person chose not to be told about again: still true of the slice. Explain a finding from its words, never from a cause you supply.
245	
246	A warning is my statement about the slice, not proof the print will fail. Say what it risks for this print and its purpose. Some are the person's call because they trade the look, the time or their hardware: adding support (marks on the surface, clean-up), re-orienting, a filament pairing, a nozzle. A setting that only shapes the toolpath is yours to settle when the request covers it.
247	
248	A finding counts as resolved only when a new slice no longer reports it, never because a setting changed or a chip was closed or ignored. Asked whether the print is ready, name what the slice still warns about.
249	
250	| What I report while slicing | What it usually means, and where it points |
251	| --- | --- |
252	| "It seems object … has floating regions / floating cantilever / large overhangs. Please re-orient the object or enable support generation."; "Your model needs support!" | Parts start in the air or overhang far and will sag or fail without support. Support settings (enable_support, support_type) or orientation; which one is the person's call. |
253	| "Object can't be printed for empty layer between … and …" (critical) | Layers with nothing printed: features thinner than a line, or a broken mesh. object_analyze, object_repair, layer height or line widths. |
254	| "… is too close to others, and collisions may be caused."; "Prime Tower is too close to others"; "too close to exclusion area" | The nozzle or head may hit an object, the tower or a no-go area. Position (object_place, plate_layout) or print_sequence. |
255	| "Found gcode path conflicts between object … and …" (critical) | Two objects' paths cross. Spacing or layout. |
256	| "A G-code path goes beyond the plate boundaries." / "… beyond the max print height." | A skirt, brim, support or the object itself leaves the printable area by a little (more is a blocker). Position, brim_width, skirt_distance or size. |
257	| Speed, acceleration, jerk or junction deviation above the printer's maximum ("JusPrin will automatically cap …"); "Setting the jerk speed too low …" | It prints with the motion capped to the printer's limits. The setting named, or the printer's machine_max_* values. |
258	| A support base pattern replaced ("Rectilinear will be used instead") | I substitute a pattern this support type cannot use. support_base_pattern. |
259	| "Partial flushing volume set to 0 …"; "PLA and PETG filaments detected in the mixture …"; filament shrinkage or XY size compensation "will not be used"; different nozzle and filament diameters with the prime tower | Multi-filament settings: colours may mix, or a setting is dropped for this plate. Flushing volumes, the filaments chosen, the setting named. |
260	| "The nozzle hardness required by the filament is higher than …" | An abrasive filament wears the installed nozzle. Physical, the person's call: nozzle or filament. |
261	| Traditional timelapse "may cause surface imperfections"; smooth timelapse with the prime tower off | Only for a print that records a timelapse, which is chosen when it is sent; the chip does not count these. timelapse_type, enable_prime_tower. |
262	
263	When the person describes a problem in a print they made that points at the filament or its settings (stringing, gaps, bulging corners), the calibration skill says whether a test is worth it.
264	
265	I raise no slicing warning for thin walls, bridges, bed adhesion, brims or cooling. When the person asks about those, read slice_report's firstLayer, islands and supports sections and the settings; never present one as a warning I gave.
266	=== /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/connection-skill/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/AgentUI/src/skills/project-setup-changed/SKILL.md
267	---
268	name: project-setup-changed
269	trigger: setup_changed
270	---
271	
272	# Checking the setup against the print's purpose
273	
274	The rules above still apply. What this print is for, or the setup it is printed with, has just changed. Before going on, check that the printer, filament, process and plate type in force suit the purpose recorded now.
275	
276	- A preset's name does not prove its values. Read the settings in force with settings_get, including the objects' own overrides.
277	- Find compatible presets with presets_list, and check the plate type against the filament with printer_setup_preview.
278	- A material and its saved filament preset are separate choices.
279	- Keep customizations that still fit. When something does not, say why and recommend a material, a compatible preset or a focused setting change.
280	- When several changes follow, record the plan, with what is unverified, using setup_record first.
281	- After the person restores an earlier version, the plan may be stale: check the setup again rather than assume it.
282	- When the workspace's plan needs reassessment (the app marks it after an import, an edit by hand, Undo or a restore), the setup card shows the mark until you record the plan again with setup_record; nothing clears it automatically. If the plan holds, or you made the change it needs, record it in this reply: a new headline if the change alters how you will print, the same headline if not. If you recommend something the person has to choose, such as switching back, record nothing yet: record the plan in the reply to their answer, so the mark stays while the choice is open.
283	=== /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/connection-skill/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/AgentUI/src/skills/slice-failed/SKILL.md
284	---
285	name: slice-failed
286	trigger: slice_failed
287	---
288	
289	# A slice that did not succeed
290	
291	The rules above still apply. A plate in the workspace now has a sliceFailure (slice_start's reason is the same record): its kind, my message, and the setting and objectId when I named them. It stays on the plate until the plate slices, so after a change it still shows the old reason until the next slice.
292	
293	The kind says what happened. "refused": I checked the plate before slicing and started nothing, so there is no slice, no estimate and nothing to print, and slicing again unchanged is refused the same way. Change what the message names (a setting, or the filament and plate pairing), then slice again. "failed": the run started and stopped with an error partway; there is no usable slice either, and the message comes from the step that failed.
294	
295	My check alone has about 150 distinct messages, many specific to a printer, a filament or a mode. Do not assume you know what one means beyond what it says, or why it happened. Read it this way: the setting key names what to change, and settings_search or settings_get with that key tells what the setting is and its value now; the object, when named, narrows where; with neither, it is about the plate or the setup as a whole. My message is the reason; do not add a different one. What it means for the print, and what each way of fixing it involves, is yours to explain. The check stops at the first problem it finds, so a slice after a fix may be refused for a different reason.
296	
297	A problem counts as fixed only when a new slice no longer reports it. A change is a step towards that, not proof of it.
298	
299	The filament and the build plate are physical things the person owns and loads, so when a message pairs them, which one to change is their decision; say what each choice means for the print. A setting that only shapes the toolpath (a layer height the nozzle can print, a support option, a line width) is yours to settle when the request covers it.
300	
301	| Message family in my check | What it usually means |
302	| --- | --- |
303	| Layer height or a line width against the nozzle ("Layer height cannot exceed nozzle diameter", "Too large line width") | A process setting does not fit the selected nozzle; the setting key names which. |
304	| "Plate N: <plate type> is not suggested for use printing filament N (<filament>)" | That filament's preset has no bed temperature for this plate type; no setting is named. A different plate type or a different filament fixes it; the bed temperature the message offers instead prints a material on a surface its maker does not recommend. |
305	| An object that exceeds the build volume or its height, or collides with another when printed one by one | Size, position or print order; the object is named. Scaling changes the object the person wanted. |
306	| Organic or tree support diameters, support base patterns, support enforcers with support off | Support settings that contradict each other or are missing; the key names the one to change. |
307	| Prime tower requirements, an extruder the printer does not have, nozzles of different diameters | Multi-material or multi-extruder settings that disagree across objects or with the printer. |
308	| Spiral vase with more than one material, a smooth timelapse or clumping detection with the "by object" sequence | Two modes that cannot run together; one has to go, and which is the person's choice when it changes what they asked for. |
309	| Speed, acceleration or jerk above the printer's maximum; precise Z height or clumping detection beside the prime tower | Warnings, not refusals: I slice anyway, capping the motion values. |
310	// What the model is told in the printer panel: its system prompt. Written
311	// here, on the page, and handed to the app with printer_instructions; the app
312	// sends it with every request of the session and offers every printer tool,
313	// and the settings tools, in every session, refusing a call that does not fit
314	// it (PrinterConversation::preflight_tool). The facts it states -- the printer list, what is on the
315	// network, the printer this is about -- come from the app in the session's
316	// `context`.
317	//
318	// The rules come in sections, and a session is sent the ones it can reach:
319	// finding and adding a printer only while adding one, so a conversation about
320	// a printer the person already has is not steered toward adding it; changing,
321	// connecting and finishing in every session, since each can lead to them.
322	// Adding a printer has its own goal; sessions about a printer the person
323	// already has get a different goal and scope. Words meant for one session
324	// reach the others when they share a section, so check every case
325	// in tests/printer_prompt, not only the one an edit is for.
